  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 313 AM CDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nMid-level water vapor imagery this morning shows a large upper ridge \nover the southern Plains, troughing over the northwestern US with \nsouthwesterly flow extending from Baja California to the Dakotas. \nWith most of the mid-level vorticity maxima embedded in the \naforementioned southwesterly flow staying well west of eastern \nKansas, the area continues to be largely influenced by the upper \nridge that remains settled across the southern Plains. This will \ncontinue to be the trend today as another afternoon of near-record \nhigh temperatures spread across eastern Kansas. Southwesterly flow \nand efficient diurnal mixing should bring down very warm 850mb \ntemperatures and help for afternoon highs to top out in the mid to \nupper 90s - close to the NBM 90th percentile. Some low-end PoPs may \nbe realized late this evening across north-central KS, but better \nchances for widespread precipitation chances remain further out west \nand north of the area.\n\nMore notable change in this pattern looks to come Saturday as a \nbetter push of vorticity associated with the trough out west moves \nover the central Rockies. This should push out a surface trough and \nfront across Kansas Saturday morning and afternoon. Scattered \nshowers and a few weak thunderstorms can be expected near the \nboundary through the morning hours but by the afternoon, \nthunderstorm activity should become more notable as inhibition \ndecreases. An environment conducive for marginally-severe storms \nlooks to set up across the area, especially near the I-35 corridor. \nMain hazards given the parameters warrant damaging winds and hail up \nto 1.25\" being the main concerns. Locally heavy rainfall will also \npose a small flooding concern given PWATs nearing the 99th \npercentile for this time of year. Scattered rain chances continue \ninto Sunday as the main trough axis pivots across Nebraska keeping \nmodest mid-level lift and moisture over the region. Outside the \nprecipitation chances, the most notable takeaway with this system as \nwe head into next week will be the cooler and more fall-like \ntemperatures. Highs Monday and Tuesday will likely stay near the low \n70s and upper 60s with lows bottoming out near the 50s and low 60s. \nPrecipitation chances should stay out of the area for the most part \nuntil low-level ridging moves east and returns southerly flow by \nThursday. The extended ensemble guidance depicts frequent waves \ntransgressing the central US and thus keeping temperature spreads in \nthe 70s for the remainder of the month.",
